I have the suspicion that this will be a very unpopular idea, but I think it
would be nice to have a configurable function where nethack will *ignore*
keypresses for user-defined interval after the last keypress. You could have
this feature triggered on the same events that cause characters to stop
waiting.

So on a cleared level, you can go zooming around all you want, and then
suddenly, your character stops. The game won't respond to keypresses for 5
seconds (unless it's like #quit or whatever), getting you into
look-before-you-leap mode.

Someone on this newsgroup metions how nethack is a game like chess -- every
move counts. I disagree. Nethack is like schizophrenic chess -- for most of
the game, what keys you press doesn't matter. You could go 1000s of turns
without anything serious happening. Then boom! All of a sudden, you are in a
situation where EVERY KEYPRESS MATTERS. If you play nethack like Every
Keypress Matters, my hat is off to you. You have more patience than a Zen
Master. So I disagree that NH is like chess. In chess, truly every move
matters. In NH, there's too much mind-numbing nothingness to have the
attitude that every move matters, because most of them really don't. This
makes a NH player have to switch between two gameplay modes. For me, it's
jarring and I don't like it. That's just me though. It's probably what
others like about NH.

I know that most hardcore nethackers will say that it's the users'
responsibility to be disciplined with the keyboard. Well phooey. Maybe it
is. However, I just have to put this out there. It's like training wheels
for dungeon crawling. Maybe add "newbie mode" like Wizard mode.



